  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday morning through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 124 PM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nA pattern change is expected into the late week/early weekend as we \nsee moisture continue to spread into the area along with a frontal \nboundary pushing in over the weekend. PWATs are currently forecast \nin the 1.5-2.5\" this weekend which IF it came to fruition would be \nwell above the 90th percentile. Although there does still continue \nto be some model spread, models are beginning to converge on a wet \npattern for this weekend. Expecting showers to begin on late Friday \nnight and continuing through Monday morning with the main windows \nfor heaviest rainfall likely between Saturday evening and Sunday \nevening. The spread will really come into play when it comes to \nrainfall amounts with this system. The NBM 10th percentile shows \nbetween a tenth and a quarter inch of precip totals through the \nweekend whereas the NBM 90th shows 3 to 4\". Do think that as we get \ncloser to the event, the totals will come more into line with a \nrainy Saturday and Sunday. One factor in all of this and potentially \nhigher totals will be the wedge. Expecting the wedge to begin \npushing into the area Saturday into Sunday which can be known to \nenhance rainfall totals in this area. Would not be surprised to see \nhigher amounts along I-85 as a result. Lightning is likely in the \nform of stratiform lightning within the showers but it's unlikely \nthat we will see severe weather with this event. The setup is not \nconducive to a severe pattern.\n\nTemperatures will also be in question as model spread is Grantley \n10-15 degrees with NBM 50th notably above the ensemble mean on \nSaturday. Models can struggle with the wedge pattern so elected to \nlower the high temps Saturday a tad to account for this and go with \nthe lower end of guidance. What all this means is that Saturday and \nSunday's highs dominated by the wedge are likely to push below \nnormal with mid 70s on Sunday. \n\nAfter this weekend, the front will push south relegating the rain \nchances to central Georgia for the week with temperatures remaining \nin the mid 70s through the week ahead.",
